    "math_academy_id": "q-49885",
    "knowledge_point_id": "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547",
    "knowledge_point": "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration",
    "problem": "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=4{x}^{2}-x$ and $y=2-{x}^{2}-4x?$",
    "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/913ec93445f3ed571fcf98ad762eba2a1bc657dce7442c8e48fc4d5f36817def.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-x & =2-{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-2)}}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm 7}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/77493917a13269c78acef8ff6fe57b5f350020542805e316f82f7a17dabedd24.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4{x}^{2}-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-{x}^{2}-4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}[\\,(2-{x}^{2}-4x)-(4{x}^{2}-x)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}(-5{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
